  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOne more upper level disturbance/shortwave will cross the region\ntoday with the track of this shortwave more across northern\nareas. This will bring some additional isolated to scattered\nshowers mainly for areas north of I-90 today into tonight. As\nthe shortwave departs, a westerly breeze will pick up again for\nthe afternoon and evening hours with some gusts on the order of\n20 to locally 35 mph, similar to previous days. As this \ndisturbance departs, some localized light lake effect showers \nare expected into early Wednesday before ending with these \nshowers mainly affecting far northern areas as well as the \nwestern Mohawk Valley into Schoharie County. Much of Wednesday \nlooks dry with a partly to mostly cloudy sky. Highs today and \nWednesday will reach the 50s to lower 60s except mid to upper \n40s across the Adirondacks and southern Greens. Lows tonight \nfall back into the lower 30s to lower 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe overall synoptic pattern for later this week is becoming a\nbit more clear with trends toward seeing at least a widespread \nlight to moderate precipitation event. An upper level trough \nwill build across the eastern half of the CONUS on Thursday with\nmultiple shortwaves rotating around the base of the trough. An \ninitial area of low pressure will approach from the Ohio Valley \non Thursday which will primarily bring a period of rain to the \nregion. By Thursday night, an initial shortwave rounding the \nbase of the trough will reach the mid-Atlantic coast and take on\na negative tilt. This will support the primary inland low to \nweaken and develop a coastal low (Nor'easter) off the coast. \nWhile there is still some uncertainty with the track and timing \nof this coastal low, it does look progressive as it tracks \nnortheastward up the coast toward Atlantic Canada by Friday. \nHowever, a secondary shortwave may take on a negative tilt off \nthe mid- Atlantic coast later Friday which may induce another \ncoastal low to form taking a similar path to the first, though \nthis secondary low development is less clear. All in all, this \nfavors periods of precipitation across most of the region later \nthis week into this weekend. Initial thermal profiles will favor\nmostly rain but as colder air gradually filters in from the \nwest, precipitation will likely mix with and change over to wet \nsnow, especially across the higher elevations. Valley areas \nlikely remain rain through Friday with a possible mix or \nchangeover to snow by this weekend. However, by this weekend, \nprecipitation intensity may be fairly light that accumulations \nwould be unlikely for valley areas, especially on paved \nsurfaces. Guidance does favor some light snowfall amounts across\nthe higher elevations (especially above 1500 feet). Trends will\ncontinue to be monitored over the next few days with additional\nadjustments to the forecast likely as details on this event \nbecome even more clear. This system looks to fully depart the \narea by Sunday with just a few lingering lake effect and lake \nenhanced/upslope snow showers mainly for areas outside of the \nHudson Valley.",
